  <h1>Basic HFT Concepts</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Why this matters for <strong>HFT_practice</strong>: the repo is named "HFT" but most
    of its Python is daily-frequency portfolio rebalancing. The <em>actual</em> HFT muscle
    lives in <code>fpga/market_maker.vhd</code> and the C++ ONNX path in
    <code>gradient_boosting/</code>. To grow either side, you need the vocabulary that
    professional HFT systems are built on.
  </p>

  <!-- ===================== §1 CONCEPT ===================== -->
  <h2>1. What HFT actually is</h2>

  <p>
    <strong>High-Frequency Trading (HFT)</strong> is a style of automated trading
    distinguished by three things, in order of importance:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Latency</strong> — measured in microseconds (µs) or nanoseconds (ns), not seconds.</li>
    <li><strong>High order rates</strong> — thousands to millions of messages per second per strategy.</li>
    <li><strong>Short holding periods</strong> — positions opened and closed inside a day, often within seconds.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    The "high frequency" refers to the rate of <em>orders</em>, not the rate of profitable
    trades. A typical HFT firm sends far more order cancels than fills.
  </p>

  <h3>The vocabulary HFT is built on</h3>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Term</th><th>What it means</th></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Bid</strong></td><td>The highest price a buyer is currently willing to pay.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Ask (Offer)</strong></td><td>The lowest price a seller is currently willing to accept.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Spread</strong></td><td><code>ask − bid</code>. The cost of crossing the book.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Mid-price</strong></td><td><code>(bid + ask) / 2</code>. A common "fair value" reference.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Limit Order Book (LOB)</strong></td><td>The full list of resting buy/sell orders at every price level.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Limit order</strong></td><td>"I will buy at X or lower." Rests in the book. Passive.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Market order</strong></td><td>"Fill me now at the best price." Crosses the spread. Aggressive.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Tick</strong></td><td>A single market data update (a trade, a quote change, a cancel).</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Tick-to-trade</strong></td><td>Time from receiving a market tick to your order leaving your NIC.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Market maker</strong></td><td>Strategy that posts both a bid and an ask, earning the spread.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Colocation</strong></td><td>Renting rack space inside the exchange's building to cut wire latency.</td></tr>
  </table>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>The single most important sentence in HFT:</strong>
    a market maker quotes <em>both sides</em> of the book and tries to capture the
    spread on round-trips. Everything in <code>fpga/market_maker.vhd</code> is in service of that.
  </div>

  <h3>Latency scale (memorize this)</h3>
  <table>
    <tr><th>System</th><th>Typical tick-to-trade</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Python script over the internet (you, today)</td><td>~50–500 <em>milliseconds</em></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Tuned CPU C++ in colocation</td><td>~500 ns – 5 µs</td></tr>
    <tr><td>FPGA in colocation (top firms)</td><td>~100–300 ns tick-to-trade</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p class="small">
    Source: industry summaries for 2025 cite FPGA tick-to-trade in the 100–500 ns range,
    vs. 500–1000 µs for CPU systems. That's a ~1000× gap, which is exactly why
    <code>market_maker.vhd</code> exists in this repo even though the Python is daily-frequency.
  </p>

  <!-- ===================== §2 MINIMAL EXAMPLE ===================== -->
  <h2>2. A minimal market-maker, in plain Python</h2>
  <p>
    Forget FPGAs for a moment. Here is the <em>logic</em> a market maker runs every time
    a new tick arrives. Read every comment.
  </p>

<pre><span class="com"># minimal_market_maker.py — illustrative; not for real trading</span>

<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">quote</span>(bid: <span class="ty">float</span>, ask: <span class="ty">float</span>, my_spread: <span class="ty">float</span>):
    <span class="com"># 1. "Fair value" reference: midpoint of the current book.</span>
    mid = (bid + ask) / <span class="num">2</span>

    <span class="com"># 2. We want to sit INSIDE the current spread to be at the top of the queue.</span>
    <span class="com">#    Our buy quote sits below mid, our sell quote sits above mid,</span>
    <span class="com">#    separated by `my_spread` (the edge we want to earn).</span>
    my_bid = mid - my_spread / <span class="num">2</span>
    my_ask = mid + my_spread / <span class="num">2</span>

    <span class="com"># 3. If we get filled on BOTH sides we pocket roughly `my_spread` per share,</span>
    <span class="com">#    minus exchange fees and minus inventory risk.</span>
    <span class="kw">return</span> my_bid, my_ask

<span class="com"># Example: book shows bid=100.00, ask=100.04. We want a 2-cent edge.</span>
buy, sell = <span class="fn">quote</span>(<span class="num">100.00</span>, <span class="num">100.04</span>, my_spread=<span class="num">0.02</span>)
<span class="com"># buy  = 100.01   (better than current bid → top of queue on buy side)</span>
<span class="com"># sell = 100.03   (better than current ask → top of queue on sell side)</span>
</pre>

  <p>
    That's it. The math is trivial. <strong>The hard part of HFT is doing this
    arithmetic, plus risk checks, plus order submission, in under a microsecond,
    on every tick, on every symbol, every day.</strong>
  </p>

  <!-- ===================== §3 IN THIS PROJECT ===================== -->
  <h2>3. In this project</h2>
  <p>
    Now look at the same logic, in VHDL, running on an FPGA.
  </p>
  <span class="filepath">fpga/market_maker.vhd (lines 93–117, condensed)</span>
<pre><span class="kw">if</span> valid_in = <span class="str">'1'</span> <span class="kw">then</span>
    <span class="com">-- (bid + ask) / 2  → mid price.  resize(_, 33) avoids overflow.</span>
    bid_plus_ask <= resize(bid_price_in, <span class="num">33</span>) + resize(ask_price_in, <span class="num">33</span>);
    mid_price    <= resize(
                        shift_right(
                            resize(bid_price_in, <span class="num">33</span>) + resize(ask_price_in, <span class="num">33</span>),
                            <span class="num">1</span>),
                        <span class="num">32</span>);

    <span class="com">-- spread / 2  → half-spread.  Arithmetic right shift = divide by 2.</span>
    half_spread  <= shift_right(spread_in, <span class="num">1</span>);

    <span class="com">-- buy quote  = mid − half_spread</span>
    <span class="com">-- sell quote = mid + half_spread</span>
    buy_price_r  <= ... - shift_right(spread_in, <span class="num">1</span>);
    sell_price_r <= ... + shift_right(spread_in, <span class="num">1</span>);
<span class="kw">end if</span>;
</pre>

  <p>Map it back to the Python:</p>
  <div class="grid2">
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Python you just read</h4>
      <p><code>mid = (bid + ask) / 2</code></p>
      <p><code>my_bid = mid - my_spread/2</code></p>
      <p><code>my_ask = mid + my_spread/2</code></p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>VHDL doing the same thing</h4>
      <p><code>bid_plus_ask + resize → /2</code> via <code>shift_right(_, 1)</code></p>
      <p><code>buy_price_r &lt;= mid - half_spread</code></p>
      <p><code>sell_price_r &lt;= mid + half_spread</code></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    Two things to notice:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Q16.16 fixed-point.</strong> The header says
      <code>real_value = integer_signal / 65536</code>. Why not floating point?
      Because float DSP blocks burn cycles and silicon. Integer + shift is one cycle.
      In HFT, every cycle is a position in the order queue.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>1 clock-cycle latency</strong> (line 14 of the header). On a 250 MHz
      clock, that's 4 ns. Compare to the Python interpreter doing the same arithmetic:
      ~hundreds of ns just for the function call overhead.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Meanwhile in the Python world, <code>paper_trading_sim.py</code> trains a
    <code>GradientBoostingRegressor</code> on daily OHLCV bars from yfinance, ranks
    tomorrow's expected returns, and rebalances <em>once per day</em>. That's not HFT —
    it's quantitative daily allocation. The C++ ONNX path in
    <code>gradient_boosting/main.cpp</code> is the bridge: same model, but loaded into
    a low-latency runtime so it <em>could</em> sit next to the FPGA logic one day.
  </p>

  <div class="callout warn">
    <strong>Repo honesty:</strong> calling this project "HFT_practice" is aspirational.
    The Python is daily/quant; the FPGA + C++ are the genuine HFT building blocks. Knowing
    which is which will save you from misnaming things in interviews and PRs.
  </div>

  <!-- ===================== §4 OFTEN CONFUSED WITH ===================== -->
  <h2>4. Often confused with</h2>

  <div class="grid2">
    <div class="card">
      <h4>HFT vs. Algorithmic Trading</h4>
      <p>
        All HFT is algorithmic, but not all algorithmic trading is HFT.
        Algo trading just means "decisions made by code." HFT additionally
        demands <em>microsecond-grade latency</em>. Your
        <code>paper_trading_sim.py</code> is algorithmic; it is not HFT.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>HFT vs. Day Trading</h4>
      <p>
        A day trader holds for minutes-to-hours and clicks a mouse. HFT holds for
        milliseconds-to-seconds and never touches a mouse. Both close positions
        intraday — that's where the confusion comes from.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Market Making vs. Stat-Arb</h4>
      <p>
        <strong>Market making</strong> posts both sides and earns the spread; it is
        directionally neutral and worries about inventory.
        <strong>Stat-arb</strong> takes a directional view (long X, short Y) based on
        a statistical signal. Your model in <code>predict_next.py</code> is closer to
        stat-arb / momentum than to market making.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Limit Order vs. Market Order</h4>
      <p>
        A <strong>limit order</strong> says "this price or better, otherwise wait" — it
        rests in the book, costs no spread, may never fill. A <strong>market order</strong>
        says "fill now" — it pays the spread, always fills (if liquidity exists).
        Market makers post limits; takers send markets.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Latency vs. Throughput</h4>
      <p>
        <strong>Latency</strong> = time for one message to traverse the pipeline.
        <strong>Throughput</strong> = messages per second the pipeline can handle.
        HFT firms care about <em>both</em>, but the marketing number quoted in
        headlines is almost always latency.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>FPGA vs. CPU</h4>
      <p>
        A CPU runs general code and pays for it with branch prediction, caches,
        and OS jitter. An <strong>FPGA</strong> is a sea of logic gates that you
        wire into a fixed pipeline at synthesis time — no OS, no cache misses, no
        instruction fetch. Deterministic latency in nanoseconds is the prize.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

