  <!-- ===================== §6 SCENARIOS ===================== -->
  <h2>6. Check your understanding (scenarios)</h2>

  <details class="scenario">
    <summary>S1. A teammate says "let's run <code>paper_trading_sim.py</code> on a colocated server to make it HFT." What do you say?</summary>
    <p>
      Colocation only helps if your bottleneck is wire latency. This script's bottleneck is
      that it makes <em>one decision per day</em> from yfinance bars. Colocation cuts microseconds
      off network hops; it does nothing for a strategy whose decision cadence is 24 hours.
      To make it "HFT," you'd need to replace the data feed, the model loop, and the execution path —
      essentially everything except the ticker list.
    </p>
  </details>

  <details class="scenario">
    <summary>S2. Why does <code>market_maker.vhd</code> have a <code>valid_in</code> input even though arithmetic could run every cycle?</summary>
    <p>
      Because not every clock cycle has a fresh price tick. <code>valid_in</code> is a
      handshake: "this cycle's <code>bid_price_in</code> / <code>ask_price_in</code> are
      meaningful — go compute." Downstream consumers watch <code>order_valid_out</code>
      to know when the buy/sell quotes are real outputs versus stale registers. This is the
      universal pattern for pipelined hardware: every meaningful datum carries a valid bit.
    </p>
  </details>

  <details class="scenario">
    <summary>S3. Why might a market maker prefer to <em>cancel</em> a quote rather than let it fill?</summary>
    <p>
      Because a fill that happens right before the mid-price moves against you is called
      <strong>adverse selection</strong> — the other side knew something you didn't. If your
      tick-to-trade is too slow to update quotes when the market shifts, you become a
      systematic loser to faster participants. That's why HFT firms cancel <em>far</em> more
      orders than they execute, and why nanoseconds of cancel latency are existential.
    </p>
  </details>

  <p class="footer-note">
